                    <td>50.8%</td>
                    <td>Blitz - Near parity</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="highlight-box">
            <strong>Key Insight:</strong> Faster time controls (bullet and blitz) tend to show larger white advantages, likely because positional understanding—a black's stronger suit in classical formats—has less time to manifest. At 10+0 and 5+8, white wins over 53% of games.
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Rating Dynamics -->
    <div class="section">
        <h2>3. Rating Dynamics and Their Impact on Outcomes</h2>

        <div class="kpi-grid">
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="kpi-value">+7.8</div>
                <div class="kpi-label">Avg. White Rating Advantage</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="kpi-value">~85%</div>
                <div class="kpi-label">Win Rate at +300 Rating</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3>The Rating Differential Effect</h3>
        <p>Rating differential between white and black is the most powerful predictor of game outcome in the dataset. When white is rated higher than black, white wins more often—but the relationship is symmetric: black also wins more often when rated higher than white.</p>

        <div class="chart-container">
            <div class="chart-title">Figure 4: White Win Rate by Rating Differential (White Rating - Black Rating)</div>
            ''' + charts['rating_diff'] + '''
        </div>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Rating Differential</th>
                    <th>White Win Rate</th>
                    <th>Interpretation</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Black > White by 300+</td>
                    <td>17.5%</td>
                    <td>Black heavily favored</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Black > White by 200-300</td>
                    <td>27.5%</td>
                    <td>Black favored</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Black > White by 100-200</td>
                    <td>36.2%</td>
                    <td>Black moderately favored</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Rating within 100 points</td>
                    <td>47.7% - 56.4%</td>
                    <td>Near parity</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>White > Black by 100-200</td>
                    <td>67.1%</td>
                    <td>White moderately favored</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>White > Black by 200-300</td>
                    <td>77.7%</td>
                    <td>White heavily favored</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>White > Black by 300+</td>
                    <td>85.1%</td>
                    <td>White overwhelmingly favored</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Rating Distribution</h3>
        <p>Player ratings in this dataset span from approximately 800 to 2700, with averages near 1597 (white) and 1589 (black). The distribution is approximately normal with a slight concentration around the 1500-1600 range, representing "club player" level.</p>

        <div class="stat-row">
            <div class="stat-item">
                <div class="stat-value">1,597</div>
                <div class="stat-label">Average White Rating</div>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-item">
                <div class="stat-value">1,589</div>
                <div class="stat-label">Average Black Rating</div>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-item">
                <div class="stat-value">~60.5</div>
                <div class="stat-label">Avg. Game Length (turns)</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Opening Analysis -->
    <div class="section">
        <h2>4. Opening Analysis: Popular Openings and Their Success Rates</h2>

        <h3>Top 15 Most Played Openings</h3>
        <div class="chart-container">
            <div class="chart-title">Figure 5: Most Frequently Played Openings (by ECO Code)</div>
            ''' + charts['top_openings'] + '''
        </div>

        <h3>Win Rates by Opening</h3>
        <p>The following chart shows win rates for the top 10 most-played openings. A white win rate above 50% indicates white performed better than average in that opening; below 50% indicates black performed better.</p>

        <div class="chart-container">
            <div class="chart-title">Figure 6: Win Rates for Top 10 Openings (by Color)</div>
            ''' + charts['opening_winrates'] + '''
        </div>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>ECO</th>
                    <th>Games</th>
                    <th>White Win %</th>
                    <th>Black Win %</th>
                    <th>Edge</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>A00 (Van't Kruijs)</td>
                    <td>1,007</td>
                    <td>39.5%</td>
                    <td>56.6%</td>
                    <td style="color: #e74c3c;">Black +17.1%</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>A40 (English)</td>
                    <td>618</td>
                    <td>54.7%</td>
                    <td>42.2%</td>
                    <td style="color: #27ae60;">White +12.5%</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>B00 (Nimzowitsch)</td>
                    <td>611</td>
                    <td>59.7%</td>
                    <td>35.7%</td>
                    <td style="color: #27ae60;">White +24.0%</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>B01 (Scandinavian)</td>
                    <td>716</td>
                    <td>50.0%</td>
                    <td>46.4%</td>
                    <td>Slight White +3.6%</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>A45 (Indian Game)</td>
                    <td>252</td>
                    <td>40.5%</td>
                    <td>53.2%</td>
                    <td style="color: #e74c3c;">Black +12.7%</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>B06 (Modern)</td>
                    <td>176</td>
                    <td>46.0%</td>
                    <td>50.0%</td>
                    <td>Slight Black +4.0%</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>B07 (Pirc Defense)</td>
                    <td>304</td>
                    <td>55.6%</td>
                    <td>38.5%</td>
                    <td style="color: #27ae60;">White +17.1%</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="highlight-box">
            <strong>Notable Finding - The Nimzowitsch Paradox:</strong> B00 (Nimzowitsch Defense) shows the largest white advantage among top openings at 59.7% white win rate. This hypermodern opening where black strikes early in the center is often considered a surprise weapon for black—but the data suggests white's extra tempo more than compensates, at least at the club level.
        </div>

        <div class="highlight-box">
            <strong>Notable Finding - A00 Underperformance:</strong> A00 (Van't Kruijs Opening, 1.e3) paradoxically shows the worst white performance—only 39.5% white wins. This ultra-solid but passive opening may give black easy equality and the ability to seize the center without compensation.
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Time Control Patterns -->
    <div class="section">
        <h2>5. Time Control Patterns and Game Characteristics</h2>

        <h3>Distribution by Time Control</h3>
        <div class="chart-container">
            <div class="chart-title">Figure 7: Game Distribution by Time Control</div>
            ''' + charts['time_control'] + '''
        </div>

        <p>Bullet (1+0) and blitz (5+0 to 15+0) formats dominate the dataset, together accounting for over 90% of all games. The 10+0 format alone represents 38.5% of all games recorded.</p>

        <h3>Game Length by Victory Status</h3>
        <div class="chart-container">
            <div class="chart-title">Figure 8: Average Game Length by How the Game Ended</div>
            ''' + charts['game_length'] + '''
        </div>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Victory Status</th>
                    <th>Average Turns</th>
                    <th>Notes</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Checkmate (mate)</td>
                    <td>Longest</td>
                    <td>Full games reaching a tactical conclusion</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Resignation (resign)</td>
                    <td>Moderate</td>
                    <td>Typically 40-60 turns when one side gives up</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Timeout (outoftime)</td>
                    <td>Variable</td>
                    <td>Often short—clock pressure leads to flagging</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Draw</td>
                    <td>Shortest</td>
                    <td>Often theoretical draws or mutual agreement</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="highlight-box">
            <strong>Time Pressure and Checkmate:</strong> Games ending in checkmate average the longest, confirming that decisive tactical games require more moves. Resignations typically occur earlier once a position becomes strategically hopeless. Timeouts are often short, indicating clock desperation rather than prolonged struggle.
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Key Findings and Takeaways -->
    <div class="section">
        <h2>6. Key Findings and Strategic Takeaways</h2>

        <div class="key-finding">
            <strong>1. The first-move advantage is real but modest.</strong> White wins 52.34% of decisive games—a 2.34 percentage point edge. This advantage varies by rating (strongest at club level, weakest at elite level) and time control (larger in bullet/blitz).
        </div>

        <div class="key-finding">
            <strong>2. Rating differential dwarfs color advantage as a predictor.</strong> A 300-point rating gap predicts ~85% win rate for the stronger player. For context, white's color advantage adds only ~2-4 percentage points—a 150-point rating gap would swamp this effect entirely.
        </div>

        <div class="key-finding">
            <strong>3. Opening choices have measurable but limited impact.</strong> Most common openings show white win rates between 50-55%, close to the baseline. The main exceptions are B00 (Nimzowitsch, white wins 59.7%) and A00 (Van't Kruijs, white wins only 39.5%). Players should prioritize openings that they understand rather than chasing marginal edge.
        </div>

        <div class="key-finding">
            <strong>4. Time control choice affects game dynamics significantly.</strong> Faster formats (10+0, 5+8) show larger white advantages—likely because positional understanding that helps black neutralize white's initiative has less time to manifest. Players seeking competitive equity may prefer longer time controls.
        </div>

        <div class="key-finding">
            <strong>5. Most games are decided before the endgame.</strong> With 55.6% ending via resignation and only 31.5% reaching checkmate, strategic position judgment (knowing when a position is lost) may be as important as tactical skill for win rate optimization.
        </div>

        <h3>Recommendations for Players</h3>
        <ul style="margin: 20px 0 20px 40px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>For white:</strong> Prioritize development and king safety in the opening. The 52% base win rate suggests that simply playing sound opening principles gives a small but real edge.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>For black:</strong> Choose openings that equalize quickly (like the French or Nimzowitsch when played precisely). Avoid passive setups like 1.e3 where white gets easy play.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>For both:</strong> Focus on rating improvement over opening experimentation. A 100-point rating gain is worth roughly 8-10 percentage points in win probability—far more than any opening edge.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Recommendations for Analysts and Coaches</h3>
        <ul style="margin: 20px 0 20px 40px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;">When evaluating opening repertoire quality, account for the baseline 52% white win rate—adjust for color before drawing conclusions about opening effectiveness.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 10px;">For player development, emphasize position evaluation and resignation timing—the data suggests many games are prolonged past the point of no return.</li>
            <li>Time control analysis reveals that blitz and bullet players face systematically higher variance due to white's larger edge in faster formats.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Appendix: Methodology -->
    <div class="section">
        <h2>Appendix: Methodology & Data Quality Notes</h2>

        <h3>Data Source</h3>
        <p>Dataset: 20,058 chess games from Lichess.org, originally published on Kaggle (datasnaek/chess). Games were collected via the Lichess API from users belonging to large Lichess teams.</p>

        <h3>Calculations</h3>
        <ul style="margin: 15px 0 15px 40px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>White Win Rate:</strong> Calculated as white_wins / (white_wins + black_wins) for games where winner != 'draw'</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Rating Differential:</strong> white_rating - black_rating. Positive values favor white.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;"><strong>Opening Analysis:</strong> Only openings with 100+ games included to ensure statistical significance.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Limitations</h3>
        <ul style="margin: 15px 0 15px 40px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Draws (4.7% of games) are excluded from win rate calculations. Draw rates may vary by opening and rating.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Opening classification relies on ECO codes, which may not capture subtle variation differences.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Time control analysis is limited to the most common formats in the dataset.</li>
            <li>Player ratings are those at the time of each game and may have changed during the game session.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="methodology-note" style="margin-top: 30px;">
            Report generated: April 2026 | Data source: Lichess.org via Kaggle dataset | Games analyzed: 20,058
        </p>
    </div>

</div>

</body>
</html>'''

with open('/app/output/final_report.html', 'w') as f:
    f.write(html_template)

print("Final report generated: /app/output/final_report.html")
print(f"File size: {len(html_template):,} bytes")

